two months before he died chris farley  returned to host the show that made him  famous it was hard to watch for all the  wrong reasons  in 1995 two years before farley's final  saturday night live appearance new york  magazine published an article titled  comedy isn't funny detailing the show's  dysfunction behind the scenes to make  matters worse farley graced the  magazine's cover seemingly branding him  as the cause of the show's plummeting  ratings the show was facing a pr  nightmare and the only solution was to  clean house saturday night live producer  lorne michaels whose reputation was  tarnished following the article had no  choice but to completely revamp the show  between 1994 and 1995 the show dropped  almost its entire cast including some of  its biggest stars like phil hartman and  mike myers some cast members like chris  farley and adam sandler were fired at  the end of the season the firing of  farley and sandler came as a shock  particularly to the actors themselves  sandler told the daily beast he also  detailed how the two used each other for  emotional support during this time we  got  mad together pretended  pretended we weren't sad and pretended  this  was for the best however farley's time  away from the show wouldn't be that long  although his return would come with  quite the price  farley's time following saturday night  live was the start of a burgeoning movie  career in 95 and 96 he and saturday  night live castmate david spade starred  in the comedy films tommyboy in black  sheep both of which were beloved by  audiences however not all of this  success was appealing to farley who for  years had struggled with his public  image and drug abuse as a result of his  fame according to the new york post  farley was disappointed with black sheep  but nevertheless agreed to it at a  screening of the film in new jersey  farley relapsed on alcohol after three  years of sobriety he returned to rehab  prior to filming beverly hills ninja a  movie his close friends and family tried  to convince him not to make crawley  returned to saturday night live in 1996  alongside david spade to promote black  sheep over a year later on october 25th  1997 farley was invited to host the show  unbeknownst to the cast crew and  audience at the time this hosting gig  would be farley's last appearance on tv  before he died in december 1997.  the opening of chris's saturday night  live hosting debut wasted no time in  addressing his struggles with substance  abuse the cold open features tim meadows  and lorne michael's office trying to  convince the producer to let farley host  michael's is apprehensive about letting  farley back but metals reassures him  that post rehab farley is clean the trio  is also visited by farley sponsor chevy  chase who backs up farley's claim  unfortunately farley's promise to lauren  doesn't last long as the comedian  doesn't show up for his monologue only  moments later instead he's in his old  dressing room flirting with cast member  anna gasteyer tim meadows tries to come  to the rescue as the replacement host  until michaels forces him off the stage  in place of chris rock together metals  and rock introduce farley and they start  the show for his first real sketch back  since being fired in 1995 farley plays  the producer of a morning talk show  called morning latte in this sketch will  ferrell and sherry o'terry's talk show  hosts are hysterical over an anecdote of  farley's character involving an  embarrassing cd purchase he made the  sketch grows increasingly awkward as  farley's producer ted tries to match the  energy of pharaoh and oteri's characters  and chimes in that he also met will  smith over the weekend they seem unfazed  by this news still cracking jokes about  his humiliating cd story the sketch also  contains an iconic sharia terry moment  as she emphasizes pharaoh's italian  translations which of course in italian  means affection no actually it's  abundance  what really makes the sketch however is  farley's somewhat subdued performance  it's refreshing to see the comedian  playing a character who seems almost  frustrated with how over the top he has  to be to earn the affections of these  naive daytime talk show personalities  molly shannon and farley were only ever  on the cast together for half of one  season but fortunately the two got their  chance to shine together when farley  returned to host in the second sketch of  the night shannon reprises her iconic  character mary katherine gallagher an  unpopular catholic school girl the  sketch takes place during a dance at  saint monica's catholic school farley  playing a student named mickey asks the  school's nuns to dance with him as he  doesn't have a date mary kathleen  gallagher on the other hand fawns over  another student but when mary's crush  rejects her she finds herself being axed  to dance by mickey this is certainly one  of the cutest sketches of the night and  an excellent showcase for both farley  and shannon it wouldn't be an episode of  saturday night live hosted by chris  farley without an appearance by the  motivational speaker matt foley the  character originated at second city and  was developed by bob odenkirk based on  an improvisation by farley the original  sketch is often regarded as one of the  greatest sketches in saturday night live  history and the character of matt foley  is easily one of the most famous ever  featured on the show crawley reprised  the character many times while he was on  the show although this appearance would  be his last time donning the iconic  glasses and checkered jacket in this  iteration foley is hired as a  motivational speaker for a spin cycle  class screaming at the participants as  he rips his pants and rants about living  in a van down by the river in an attempt  to motivate them the biggest laugh from  the audience in this sketch comes when  matt foley needs to take a break to  hydrate he pulls out a coffee pot from  his duffel bag and douses himself in  coffee can we continue peddling because  we're all paying for this class okay  shut your pipehole missy though this  sketch isn't as iconic as farley's first  foray with the motivational speaker it's  enough of a crowdpleaser for the  audience  following the sketch is a weekend update  segment of the show hosted by norm  mcdonald norm's tenure on saturday night  live was no stranger to controversy  particularly following a highly  publicized o.j simpson murder trial  unbeknownst to norm the farley hosted  episode would be one of his last  appearances on weekend update before he  was fired though he'd later returned to  host in 1999 as usual norm's jokes  during this episode were divisive but he  was prepared when one didn't go well  that one wasn't very good but  try to laugh anyway because it helps  farley have a little rest  sadly norm's firing would come to pass  only a few months after this episode  according to live from new york a book  about the oral history of saturday night  live lorne michaels was told to fire the  weekend update anchor by nbc president  don ulmeyer at a dinner that took place  the same night as farley's funeral norm  remained an iconic stand-up comedian  following saturday night live up until  his recent passing in september 2021 one  of farley's sketches later in the show  would come to be a historic moment for  saturday night live though audiences  wouldn't know it at the time the sketch  centers on a daytime talk show hosted by  anna gasteyer with her guest played by  sherry o'terry complaining about her  problems raising her baby the baby in  question is predictably played by farley  who serves as a force of chaos on the  show and its audience it only gets more  absurd when tim metals enters as a child  doctor only to have both of his arms  ripped off by a manic farley baby what  makes this sketch such an important  moment for saturday night live is that  according to newsweek it was the first  sketch written by tina fey to get on the  air the future 30 rock star was a newly  hired writer in the 23rd season and  would go on to make history on the show  as head writer weekend update anchor and  six time host  one of chris farley's most popular  recurring sketches during his time on  the show was as a member of the chicago  super fans the characters were often  seen in some kind of pub or sports bar  conversing about their favorite football  team the bears and their head coach mike  ditka when farley hosted in 1997 the  super fans returned for a pre-taped  sketch revolving around mike ditka's  real-life departure from the chicago  bears to join the new orleans saints and  the super fans grip with their crumbling  lives following the loss of their  beloved dick cup farley's character todd  has chosen to reject reality and  believes that it's 1986. fortunately  todd is finally able to accept reality  after receiving a phone call from the  real mike ditka it's a great final  showcase for one of farley's most  memorable recurring sketches at the same  time a bit where farley's character  suffers a heart attack does come across  as hard to watch  despite chris farley's episode being a  welcome return for the saturday night  live legend audiences could tell that  the comedian was in a worsening  condition his voice throughout the  episode was noticeably hoarse after  dress rehearsal which wasn't helped by  his appearance as characters like matt  foley which required him to strain his  vocal cords even harder in several books  detailing farley's life like live from  new york and the chris frarley show  farley's condition backstage was delved  into norm mcdonald criticized the  decision to put farley on an exercise  bike in the mad foley sketch and claimed  he tried to stretch out that episode's  weekend update to give him time to rest  in future reruns of the episode the cold  open and monologue were cut out of  respect for farley understandably the  episode is not easy to swallow anymore  especially for those who knew farley  well in the hours after farley's death  saturday night live writer al franken  stated that he had found elements of the  episode quote disturbing on december 18  1997 chris farley was found dead in his  chicago apartment after overdosing on  drugs and alcohol farley was only 33 at  the time and yet his death wasn't a  shock to his close friends and family in  live from new york bob odenkirk and  chris rock described the last time they  saw farley feeling like his fate was  inevitable others like robert smeigle  described the immense guilt they felt  over not being able to help their friend  through his struggles in retrospect many  of farley's collaborators have expressed  their distaste for farley's career  trajectory one of farley's most famous  sketches chippendales has been  criticized by former saturday night live  riders and cast members for  psychologically damaging farley  self-image in an interview with the new  yorker robert smigel regretted adding  the coffee table smashing at the end of  the original matt foley sketch  one person who has continued to spread  the good word about chris farley is adam  sandler the two were close knit on the  cast of saturday night live appearing  together in sketches like hurley he boy  and lunch ladyland however unlike farley  sandler waited a long time to return to  host the iconic late night sketch show  he finally made his hosting debut in  2019 reprising some of his iconic  characters like opera man the real big  moment of the night came at the very end  of the show right before the cast said  goodnight in the late 2010s sandler  filmed his first comedy special in years  100 fresh which ends with the song  sandless sings as a tribute to farley  sandler reprised the song on the very  stage where he and farley made some of  the best comedy of the 1990s despite the  criticism they received from nbc  higher-ups the song is equal parts  hilarious and heartwarming and the  moment was described by the cast and  crew on the episode as a tear-jerker for  the audience and sandler still it's a  touching moment to remind audiences just  how powerful of a presence chris farley  was and how underappreciated he may have  been at the time  if you or anyone you know is struggling  with addiction issues help is available  visit the substance abuse and mental  health services administration website  or contact samhsa's national helpline at  1-800-662-help  4357

